  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nThe area remains under the influence of upper ridging as we head \ninto next week, with temperatures moderating back to above average \nby midweek. Monday will be briefly cooler behind a weak cold front \nthat pushes through the area on Sunday, with weak warm advection \ndelayed until Monday evening. This leads to highs in the 60s, still \nnear average for mid-October. Stronger warm advection returns by \nTuesday, with increasing southerly winds expected through the \nafternoon hours. This boosts highs back into the upper 60s to low \n70s, with sunny skies amid ample subsidence aloft. Little change is \nexpected for Wednesday and Thursday, with temperatures \nmoderating further into the middle to upper 70s as the area \nremains under the influence of upper ridging. The upper pattern \nlooks to remain rather stagnant through much of the upcoming \nweek, with an omega block pattern evolving across the CONUS. \nLittle to no weather concerns are expected with this in mind, \nwith the increasing southerly flow allowing for modest moisture \nreturn and push dewpoints into the 50s. This precludes any \ngreater fire weather concerns, and should lead to largely \npleasant fall weather conditions across the area next week. \n\nBy late week, an upper low will begin to eject east out of the Four \nCorners region, beginning to cross the Rockies by Friday night. At \nthe surface, low pressure will quickly deepen across southeastern \nMontana. This surface low will begin to eject east into the Dakotas \nearly Saturday morning, dragging a cold front into the area Saturday \nafternoon. The timing of this frontal passage will need to be \nmonitored, as guidance suggests at least modest instability will \ndevelop ahead of the boundary. Thunderstorm development would be \npossible across the area Saturday evening, depending on frontal \nposition. This will continue to be monitored, though any severe \nthreat appears low at this time due to weak deep layer shear.",
